<div class="docs-hero">
  <h1>Recipes</h1>
  <p>Flint recipes are compositions: existing components plus a few lines of
     author CSS for layout only. Nothing here adds classes to the library —
     that keeps the component contract small and the recipes copy-pasteable.</p>
</div>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="r-grid">Card grid</h2>
  <p>The Keep-style wall of note cards: a CSS grid of <code>fl-card</code>s
     that reflows from one column to as many as fit, no media queries —
     <code>auto-fill</code> does the work. Two lines of author CSS:</p>
  {% set gridCss %}
<style>
  .fl-card-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(240px, 1fr));
    gap: var(--fl-space-2);              /* Keep's tight 8px rhythm */
  }
  .fl-card-grid .fl-card { margin-top: 0; }  /* kill the stacked-card margin */
</style>
{% endset %}
  {{ snippet(gridCss) }}
  <p>Cards that navigate or open an editor are real interactive elements —
     a <code>&lt;a class="fl-card"&gt;</code> or
     <code>&lt;button class="fl-card"&gt;</code>, never a div with a click
     handler. Note the inner elements: a <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> takes
     phrasing content only, so its title and text are <code>&lt;span&gt;</code>s
     with the card classes; the anchor (transparent content model) keeps a real
     <code>h2</code>/<code>p</code> so screen-reader heading navigation still
     finds it:</p>
  {% set gridMarkup %}
<div class="fl-card-grid">
  <button class="fl-card" type="button">
    <span class="fl-card__title">Packing list</span>
    <span class="fl-card__text">Smores, extra towel, warm socks…</span>
  </button>
  <a class="fl-card" href="/note/42">
    <h2 class="fl-card__title">Q3 roadmap</h2>
    <p class="fl-card__text">Ship the rail shell, then the menu.</p>
  </a>
</div>
{% endset %}
  {{ snippet(gridMarkup) }}
  <ul>
    <li><strong>No-JS:</strong> the grid is pure CSS; every card works as its
        native element (anchor navigates, button clicks).</li>
    <li><strong>240px floor:</strong> three cards fill a 960px reading column;
        phones get one column without a media query. Raise the floor for
        wider cards, never below ~200px.</li>
    <li><strong>Selection tint:</strong> for selectable cards combine with the
        checkbox lesson from the <a href="components/lists.html">Lists</a>
        page: <code>.fl-card:has(.fl-check:checked)</code> → tint. The
        checkbox owns the state, the card reads it.</li>
    <li><strong>Masonry is a gap, deliberately:</strong> variable-height card
        walls need <code>grid-template-rows: masonry</code>, which no engine
        ships. Equal-height rows are the honest web version.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="r-empty">Empty state</h2>
  <p>M2 has no empty-state spec; Google's apps draw it as a centered stack —
     large muted icon, one title line, one supporting line, and (optionally)
     one action. Composed from existing pieces, the author CSS is only the
     stack:</p>
  {% set emptyCss %}
<style>
  .fl-empty {
    display: grid;
    justify-items: center;
    gap: var(--fl-space-2);
    padding: var(--fl-space-4);
    text-align: center;
    color: var(--fl-text-secondary);
  }
  .fl-empty .fl-icon { width: 48px; height: 48px; border-radius: var(--fl-radius-card); }
  .fl-empty__title { margin: 0; font-size: var(--fl-fs-title); font-weight: 500; color: var(--fl-text); }
</style>
{% endset %}
  {{ snippet(emptyCss) }}
  {% set emptyMarkup %}
<div class="fl-empty">
  <span class="fl-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  <h2 class="fl-empty__title">No notes yet</h2>
  <p>Notes you add appear here.</p>
  <button class="fl-btn fl-btn--outline" type="button">Take a note</button>
</div>
{% endset %}
  {{ snippet(emptyMarkup) }}
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Announce it once:</strong> the empty state is normal content —
        no <code>aria-live</code>, no <code>role="status"</code>. It renders
        where the list would render.</li>
    <li><strong>One action maximum</strong> — the empty state is a signpost,
        not a toolbar. More than one action means the screen isn't actually
        empty.</li>
    <li><strong>Swap the placeholder</strong> for a real 48px icon when you
        have a set — the recipe doesn't change.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

